            0.159,
            0.236,
            0.768,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Why are 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, … called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "triangular numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? Why are 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "square numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "squares",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? Why are 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, … called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cubes",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.315,
            0.834,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. You will have noticed that 36 is both a triangular number and a square number! That is, 36 dots can be arranged perfectly both in a triangle and in a square. Make pictures in your notebook illustrating this!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.418,
            0.833,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This shows that the same number can be represented differently, and play different roles, depending on the context. Try representing some other numbers pictorially in different ways!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.497,
            0.764,
            0.518
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. What would you call the following sequence of numbers?"
